---
// /hosting/large-files/: D00-9, the order release assets → Zenodo → Hugging Face before Git LFS, then
// LFS itself: support and client setup, file size and quotas, over quota, the integrity check, file
// locking, moving files into and out of LFS, LFS objects in archives, and the binary and line-ending
// attributes. A static page (0 requests), science.css only; the platform's name from src/config.ts;
// every figure from src/lib/limits-text.ts.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { DATA_HOMES, GIT_DOCS, hostingFacts, SOURCES } from "../../lib/limits-text";

const t = hostingFacts();
const setup = [
  "git lfs install",
  'git lfs track "*.nii.gz"',
  "git add .gitattributes",
  'git commit -m "Track NIfTI images with Git LFS"',
];
const integrity = ["git lfs push --all origin"];
const locking = [
  'git lfs track "*.fig" --lockable',
  "git lfs lock figures/figure-a.fig",
  "git lfs locks",
  "git lfs unlock figures/figure-a.fig",
  "git lfs unlock figures/figure-a.fig --force",
];
const moveIn = ['git lfs migrate import --include="*.h5" --everything', "git push --force-with-lease --all origin"];
const moveOut = [
  'git lfs untrack "*.h5"',
  "git rm --cached data/results.h5",
  "git add data/results.h5 .gitattributes",
  'git commit -m "Keep results.h5 in git, not LFS"',
];
const attributes = [
  "# .gitattributes",
  "* text=auto",
  "*.sh text eol=lf",
  "*.bat text eol=crlf",
  "*.mat binary",
  "*.nii.gz binary",
];
---
<Base
  title="Large files"
  description="Large files and data: release assets, Zenodo, Hugging Face, then Git LFS."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Hosting code", href: "/hosting/" }, { text: "Large files" }]}
>
  <h1>Large files</h1>
  <p class="summary">
    Where large files and data go: release assets, a Zenodo record, a Hugging Face dataset, then Git LFS for small
    binaries only.
  </p>
  <p>
    GitHub blocks a file larger than {t.file} in git, and warns above {t.fileWarn}{" "}
    (<a href="/hosting/limits/">the limits</a>). Data rarely belongs in git at all: it has its own homes, which cost you
    nothing and give it a persistent identifier.
  </p>

  <h2 id="order">Where data goes, in this order</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Release assets.</strong> Attach the files to a release of your repository on GitHub: each file
      {t.releaseAsset}, up to {t.releaseAssets} files a release, with no limit on their total size or on downloads.
      Source: <a href={SOURCES.releases}>About releases</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A Zenodo record</strong>, which you make on <a href={DATA_HOMES.zenodo}>Zenodo</a>: a DOI for the data,
      cited by the paper and by the repository's README. Zenodo can also archive each release of a GitHub repository
      (<a href={DATA_HOMES.zenodoGithub}>Zenodo and GitHub</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A Hugging Face dataset</strong> in your own Hugging Face account, for data that is read in parts or
      updated (<a href={DATA_HOMES.huggingFaceDatasets}>datasets</a>,
      <a href={DATA_HOMES.huggingFaceStorage}>storage limits</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Git LFS</strong>, for small binaries the code needs next to it: a figure's source file, a trained model
      of modest size, a stimulus file. Its quotas are per account, so one popular dataset in LFS could spend a month's
      downloads for all your repositories.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>{SITE_NAME} never downloads your LFS objects or release assets itself: the downloads are counted on GitHub, as
    anyone's.</p>

  <h2 id="lfs">Git LFS support</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub supports Git LFS. Git keeps a small pointer file (a version line, the object's SHA-256 and its size) in
    place of each large file, and the file itself goes to GitHub's LFS storage. Which files are LFS files is written in
    the repository's <code>.gitattributes</code>. Sources: <a href={SOURCES.aboutLfs}>About Git LFS</a>,
    <a href={GIT_DOCS.lfs}>Git LFS</a>.
  </p>
  <h3 id="lfs-setup">Client setup</h3>
  <p>
    Install Git LFS once on each computer (<a href={SOURCES.installingLfs}>installing Git LFS</a>), then in the
    repository track a pattern and commit <code>.gitattributes</code>:
  </p>
  <pre class="commands">{setup.join("\n")}</pre>
  <p>
    Everyone who clones the repository needs Git LFS too: without it, they get the pointer files instead of the
    content. Say so in the README. Sources: <a href={SOURCES.configuringLfs}>Configuring Git LFS</a>,
    <a href={SOURCES.lfsCollaboration}>Collaboration with Git LFS</a>.
  </p>

  {t.lfs && (
    <>
      <h2 id="lfs-quotas">LFS file size and quotas</h2>
      <p>
        On GitHub Free, one LFS file is at most {t.lfsFile}; each account has {t.lfsStorage} of LFS storage and
        {" "}{t.lfsBandwidth} of LFS downloads a month, for all its repositories. A fork's LFS objects count against the
        parent repository's owner, and pushing a file again counts it in full. Sources:
        <a href={SOURCES.aboutLfs}>About Git LFS</a>, <a href={SOURCES.lfsBilling}>Git LFS billing</a>.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 id="over-quota">Over quota</h2>
  <p>
    Once a quota is spent on an account without a payment method, Git LFS is blocked for that account until the next
    month: clones get the pointer files only, and new LFS pushes are refused. With a payment method on file, GitHub
    bills the excess instead. {SITE_NAME} asks for no payment method and pays for nothing on your behalf.
    Source: <a href={SOURCES.lfsBilling}>Git LFS billing</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="integrity">Integrity check</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub refuses a push whose commits point to LFS objects that were never uploaded. Upload every object the branches
    point to, then push again:
  </p>
  <pre class="commands">{integrity.join("\n")}</pre>
  <p>Source: <a href={SOURCES.lfsUploadFailures}>Resolving Git LFS upload failures</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="locking">File locking</h2>
  <p>
    A binary file cannot be merged, so two people editing it at once lose one person's work. Git LFS can lock it: a
    pattern marked lockable is read-only in each clone until you lock the file; GitHub refuses a push that changes a
    file someone else has locked; an administrator can force an unlock.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands">{locking.join("\n")}</pre>
  <p>Source: <a href={SOURCES.lfsLocking}>Git LFS's locking</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="moving">Moving files into and out of LFS</h2>
  <p>
    Into LFS, for a file already committed: <code>git lfs migrate</code> rewrites the history so that every commit
    holds the pointer. Rewriting history has consequences for tracing maps: read
    <a href="/hosting/history/">rewriting history</a> first.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands">{moveIn.join("\n")}</pre>
  <p>Out of LFS, from now on:</p>
  <pre class="commands">{moveOut.join("\n")}</pre>
  <p>
    Objects removed from LFS still count against the storage quota: GitHub deletes them only with the repository, or
    through GitHub Support. Sources: <a href={SOURCES.movingToLfs}>Moving a file to Git LFS</a>,
    <a href={SOURCES.removingFromLfs}>Removing files from Git LFS</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="archives">LFS objects in archives</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub's "Download ZIP" and release archives hold the LFS pointer files unless the repository's setting "Include Git
    LFS objects in archives" is on. When it is, the archives hold the files, and each download counts against the LFS
    download quota. Source: <a href={SOURCES.lfsArchives}>Managing Git LFS objects in archives</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="attributes">The binary and line-ending attributes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>.gitattributes</code> also tells git how to treat text and binary files: <code>text=auto</code> lets git
    normalise line endings, <code>eol=lf</code> or <code>eol=crlf</code> fixes them for a pattern, and
    <code>binary</code> (the same as <code>-text -diff</code>) tells git never to change a file nor show it line by line.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands">{attributes.join("\n")}</pre>
  <p>
    Sources: <a href={SOURCES.lineEndings}>Configuring Git to handle line endings</a>,
    <a href={GIT_DOCS.gitattributes}>gitattributes</a>.
  </p>

  <p><a href="/hosting/">The hosting guides</a></p>
</Base>
